/** Editorial translations for siman 196 failing blocks — seven clean days / bedikot */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#ג': `Shabbat. Shach wrote that from Maharil's words it appears that even though she also prayed and they accepted Shabbat it is permitted; but the Gaon Ammo"z"l wrote for the Rav that if the prayer is Maariv one may not be lenient even bediavad; and so is Maharshal's view in a responsum (and from the Agur I cited it is proven that even for those who are lenient it is only until 24 hours; and if so when the day is so long we consider day and night equal. But after that, for all it is forbidden) end quote (and Taz wrote in name of Rashal that since our women begin counting from day 6 it is obvious she can count even after Maariv prayer on erev Shabbat).`,
    '10#א': `The contradiction. Taz wrote that even for us, who are stringent even when seeing a drop of blood like mustard, she needs seven clean days — what is the practical difference? The Mechaber wrote this law of negation; and it appears there is practical difference also now: per what we hold, a woman who has a veset need not check at all, as written beginning siman 187, for she is in presumption of purity; not so regarding seven clean days — we hold here that lechatchila she needs checking, for she is in presumption of impurity until completion of the seven clean days; therefore he informs us here that presumption of impurity is upon her until seven consecutive days are completed; lest you say that when the seven clean days end she returns to presumption of purity and need not check even lechatchila — he informs us that even if she sees at the very end of the seven she negates everything; if so, her original presumption is still upon her and she needs checking lechatchila also at that time.`,
    '10#ב': `And you know. Shach disagrees with Rama on this law and ruled that even if she knows with certainty that her wound discharges blood, nevertheless she may not attribute it in the first three days of seven clean days; and so from Bach's words — one may be lenient only specifically when she is full of boils and blood always issues from her, for then we say if she does not attribute to it she cannot ever count seven clean days; but otherwise, even if it is known her wound discharges blood, one may not be lenient end quote — he elaborates with many proofs; and Taz wrote that one should understand Rama's words as dealing with a wound she cannot guard properly — then she attributes; but whenever she can guard and did not do so, she may not attribute at all end quote (see responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 65 and Avnei Nezer siman 79).`,
    '11#ג': `Inside. Meaning, even though six onot were completed at the beginning of night four after her relations, nevertheless since she discharged at the beginning of night four before the time of her relations, as stated — if so it negates that day, namely all of day four, and she begins counting from day five end quote Shach.`,
    '11#ד': `To add. Taz wrote, and so in siman 196 in name of Maharal of Prague, that a bride after relations of mitzvah can count from day five for her relations; and his reason appears: since here there is no niddah blood, only hymeneal blood, they were not stringent regarding relations during bein hashemashot; but if she truly had relations of mitzvah during bein hashemashot, it appears certainly we consider her as if she had relations on the night after that bein hashemashot, even regarding a bride — so if the bride had relations on the night of relations of mitzvah she counts from day five; and if she had relations during bein hashemashot at the end of day one she counts from day six, as stated; and Maharshal wrote that if she saw after Maariv on day one while it is still day, she can wear clean garments at the end of day five and count from day six, and there is no doubt in the matter end quote; and from his words it appears that even though she also prayed Maariv. Shach.`,
    '11#ה': `Stain. Meaning, a pure woman who finds a stain — her law is like an actual sight also for this; but a woman who wore whites and found a stain, and even an actual sight within the days of clean days — she need not wait six onot but counts anew seven clean days from the day she can. Taz. And Shach wrote that it appears regarding a stain one may be lenient where she did not have relations, to count from the morrow of finding the stain, for this case of not having relations itself is a distant decree; therefore one should be stringent only regarding actual sight but not regarding rabbinic stains, for we follow a lenient approach in many places (and see responsum Sha'y part 2 question 77, who ruled regarding a woman who immersed and her husband was not in the city on the night of her immersion and she found a stain — he rules for that day and counts from the morrow, even though the custom is to be stringent; nevertheless in such a case, where she did not have relations and her husband is not in the city, lean toward leniency in stains; and so responsum Pnei Yehoshua siman 12 end quote); and possibly for this the Rav did not write this; and some wrote now etc. after the law of stain only after the law of sight; but nevertheless what Bach wishes, to be lenient even in actual sight when the husband is not in the city — his words do not appear correct, and so is the settled custom in these lands to be stringent even when the husband is not in the city; and greater than this, the custom that even a bride does not count seven clean days until day five of her sight, even though the groom has not yet come to the city; and even though per my view there is no reason to be stringent at all, nor is it mentioned in any early or late posek — nevertheless let it be only among permitted matters where others practiced prohibition; and nevertheless in a time of pressure one may permit a bride that immediately when she stops in purity she counts seven clean days — for thus is better than the custom that they marry while she is niddah, which Rambam ruled she should not marry at all until pure; and even though we are not meticulous so, as written in Even HaEzer siman 61 — nevertheless certainly where possible in purity is better; and Taz wrote that it appears to be lenient regarding a widow who declared niddah near her chuppah, and even after the chuppah before she secluded with her groom she does not wait at all, for in this certainly there is no reason to decree lest she had relations as if she had relations, since she has not yet entered complete relations with him — why decree such? end quote.`,
    '12#א': `One. Shach wrote, and it appears here we do not practice being stringent because of the decree of bein hashemashot, and she immerses immediately after four days and one clean day, for it is uncommon that she errs in counting; and in an uncommon matter the Sages did not decree; and Taz wrote, and to him perhaps she saw blood in those six onot but covered it — Sha"z, as written siman 193 regarding relations of mitzvah, for there is different, since blood is common from relations.`,
    '12#ב': `Time. For behold she already counted seven clean days, only lacking immersion; and it is surprising from what is written in Even HaEzer siman 13 seif 5: a convert and his wife, when they converted, separate them ninety days to distinguish between seed planted in holiness and seed planted not in holiness; and likewise we should separate her after he had relations in prohibition, to distinguish if she conceived in prohibition when she immersed improperly; and it appears here it deals with a woman whose husband was available to her before this — therefore the distinction is not relevant; but if her husband was not with her before this act within ninety days, automatically she needs separation after this forbidden relations for ninety days; and difficult practically; but transgressing the law requiring seven clean days because of anticipation, as written end siman 193, is not at all like this law, for there is only a rabbinic prohibition alone end quote (and in Nechakel Tzeddek he answered that there, since planted not in holiness he is not a complete Jew — in several laws he differs from Israel, as written siman 269; but even one born from a niddah we hold kosher end quote (and see Beit Shmuel there s.k. 15, who also sensed this)); and in Beit Yosef wrote in name of Rosh that if she immersed on night seven and had relations on day seven, also it is not a contradiction, and she immerses immediately after seven; and Shach wrote difficult practically — he wished to equate words of Semak to Rosh, and the simple meaning of Semak implies only after seven specifically end quote).`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Baraita of R. Meir, Niddah page 33a — another version: the day she stops on it, etc.; and even though it was rejected there in the Gemara, Rosh wrote the rejection is a mere rejection, and so it states there page 69a.`,
    '5#_': `Language of Rashba there in Teshuvot HaRashba, and so Tur in name of his father, and per the view of R. in the baraita there page 68b: since she stopped, she stopped. (°) Explanation: since her opening is still open.`,
    '10#_': `Like R. Eliezer there in the mishnah, and Rav there page 69a, from the conclusion of Rosh there; and so Rashba wrote in Teshuvot and Sha"z.`,
    '11#_': `Rosh there in name of Raavad, and so Rashba.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#ו': `And some say permitted, etc. Tosafot Ketubot 47a s.v. demasar, etc., and Pesachim 99b s.v. ad, etc., and Berakhot 27b — they raised a dilemma: one says Kiddush, etc., one says Havdalah, etc. — implies that for other matters not related to Shabbat it is certainly not night.`,
    '2#ב': `And bediavad, etc. According to Perishah in the mishnah only if she checked in the morning and during bein hashemashot did not separate; but during bein hashemashot according to Perishah; and as stated "if only," etc. — clearly R. Yosi does not deal in this manner, and on this the Sages speak on two, etc., and bediavad; and difficult — if so let the Sages say for themselves on the first; and so Ramban, specifically with a tight cloth all bein hashemashot, only from Tosafot there s.v. amru, etc., it appears there is no need for what he wrote; and one may say for himself, etc.; but Rambam's view appears in the first reasoning, that he ruled anonymously and wrote that checking on the first day does not help bediavad.`,
    '4#ב': `Only, etc., language. Like Rav there 69a, who holds like the Sages as stated, and "was I not established," etc., and from where do you say for the Sages, etc.`,
    '4#ו': `And the checking, etc., and bediavad. 17a Tosefta: although, etc.; Tosefta and of, etc.`,
    '6#א': `In an old garment. There 17a Lek — yes, etc., and Abaye, etc.`,
    '6#ה': `And insert it, etc., deep. For otherwise it is not checking, as stated 12a — he told him "who finds," etc., and we teach: checking of zav and zavah who checked, etc., as above.`,
    '6#ו': `For holes and cracks. As stated 5a: until before, etc., since there is disturbance, etc.`,
    '6#ז': `Until the place, etc. Rosh and Hagahot Maimoniyot and Rivash. And their reason: since it defiles in the outer chamber, as stated chapter 5, and we say there 41b in the mishnah, etc.; but Sha"z wrote only for holes and cracks, and this is the meaning; and if it is difficult, etc.; nevertheless bediavad, etc., and so in the Gemara at the end of the chapter above.`,
    '10#א': `Some say in three days, etc. For she has not yet exited her presumption; and a fortiori from zavim mishnah 2, and they agree regarding sight, etc.; and the reason of Beit Shammai is that for three days we are concerned for zivah drippings from doubt, as stated in Negaim chapter 5 and chapter 4 of Taharot at first, etc.`,
    '10#ג': `And likewise if she has, etc. For even regarding actual sight we attribute, as stated 16a, 66a, and so above siman 187 — all the more regarding rabbinic stains. D"M end quote.`,
    '13#_': `The woman, etc. Tosafot 41b s.v. poleitet, etc., and this is the meaning in Yevamot 69b: she immerses and eats, etc., and there 108a: and she immerses, etc.; but what is stated in the first version, that it goes by majority, does not help, lest something remain, as stated; and what wrote "perhaps," etc. Rosh and Sha"z.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '4#main': `Each day of the seven counting days she must check lechatchila twice each day — once in the morning and once near bein hashemashot (Tur in name of Sefer HaTaharah and responsa); and if she did not check all seven, only once — no distinction whether she checked on the first day of the seven or on the seventh day, or on one of the middle days — after she checked on the day before the seventh and found pure, it counts for her; but if she did not check all seven and on the eighth day checked and found pure, she has only day eight alone and completes for it; and some say she must check on the first day of the seven and on the seventh day, and one may not be lenient; and the checking should be by daylight and not by candlelight (Tashbetz 126 and Rashba in Teshuvot), and bediavad it helps even by candlelight (so appears in Beit Yosef).`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Shabbat. Avodat HaTashbitz wrote in name of Shach, but after that for all it is forbidden; and see in Dagul MeRevavah, who challenged this and wrote one should not be stringent at all, even lechatchila; and it appears even Shach was not stringent except when she also prayed and the congregation prayed Maariv and accepted Shabbat; and also in this it appears to be lenient bediavad end quote. And what Baer Heitev and Taz wrote in name of Rashal, etc. — see Dagul MeRevavah, who also challenged this: Maharshal deals (on Shach s.k. 19) with a sight that she saw after Maariv prayer while it is still day; and regarding when to wear whites — if on Thursday of her sight while it is still day, or Thursday from the morrow — on this he wrote that since our women begin from day six one may be lenient; but here it deals with checking of hefsek taharah — there is no difference for our women that if after Maariv prayer it is night, the morrow does not enter the count of seven clean end quote — these matters were said and repeated in his book, responsum Noda Biyhudah second part siman 131 end quote.`,
    '4#_': `Counting days. The holy Gaon, author of Shelah page 101, required that on the days of counting clean days she count each day and say "today is day," etc., as written "and she shall count to her"; but responsum Noda Biyhudah second part Yoreh Deah siman 123 disagrees; and see responsum Maharam bar Baruch, Prague edition siman 252, who also wrote she need not count, for it is not like the omer end quote; and so responsum Radvaz part 1 siman 27 also wrote she need not pronounce the number on her lips end quote — the reason.`,
    '5#_': `But if she did not check. [See Sefer Chokhmat Adam s.k. 3, who wrote that from the words of the Gemara Niddah page 69, whoever did not check — even if she did not feel — is tamei from Torah; days of clean days count, and we require checking, etc. end quote — the entire discussion; and see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 177, who disagrees and wrote the law is the true law regarding hefsek taharah: one need not evidence, for certainly what she did not feel all bein hashemashot her source was open — behold it is open and standing, and she must know her source closed — impossible to know except via checking with a white cloth; but regarding counting clean days one needs evidence, for possibly they were counting before us — all that is clear to her is that she guarded herself one morning of the seven days and knows with certainty that then her source did not open; and his proof from the Gemara does not decide at all; and he also brought in name of Sefer Avnei Meluim, who challenged on what he further wrote: that if blood issued without sensation it negates, for the source — its place is tamei — and it is like one who discharges; and this says from Torah it does not negate in any way; but for us even a stain negates; and also what Chokhmat Adam further wrote there, between a woman presumed niddah and afterward born to her a wound discharging blood, and a pure woman born to her a wound discharging blood but tamei on her onah day as above siman 187 seif 5 — since they needed tumah they do not exit tumah until checking of hefsek taharah and one day of the seven clean completely clean without sight of wound blood (brought briefly above siman 187 s.k. 31) — Chatam Sofer there elaborated to disagree, and from his words it is clear he agrees in one matter and disputes in one; and his view for halachah: a woman who was tamei niddah and before she stopped in purity her opening is still open — born to her a wound — in this Chokhmat Adam's words are correct and she has no permission to stop in purity (the examiner will understand that also in this dispute he does not fully agree to Chokhmat Adam); but a woman whose opening is closed, born to her a wound, and her onah day arrived, and we are concerned for Tosafot's view and defile her — then after so many days passed where there is no way blood draws for her, and presumably she already closed her source — she can stop in purity such that all bein hashemashot she is with distraction of mind from all thoughts and guards herself; and if she does not feel, then opening of the source stops in purity and she becomes pure end quote — in the reason of the matter; and as stated above siman 191 s.v. shelo bishe'at vesatah].`,
    '10#_': `In three days, etc. And if she counts clean days on a stain she found — if she afterward finds a stain in the first clean garments, see Even HaEzer in Beit Shmuel siman 63 s.k. 3; and see in Dagul MeRevavah, who wrote one may be lenient in this; and a fortiori one may be lenient when they urged her to marry and she reconciled and found a stain in the first three days end quote. [And see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 187: a woman who checked herself to go to synagogue, and on the day of hefsek taharah she stopped and counted, and within three days found a stain and has something to attribute — if we say that within three days we do not attribute a stain, or we consider from the day she checked to go to synagogue — already they went in this with rulings: Avnei Nezer siman 79 is lenient and Tzitz Eliezer siman 65 is stringent in his view; and in Sha"ch s.k. 33 leans to leniency, but wrote that Rashi's explanation in the mishnah opening Niddah — from deposit to deposit, morning and evening checking — Maharam of Lublin learned specifically regarding checking instituted by Chazal, but not another checking, for she does not examine well; and he brings Maharam Lublin part 3 from Moharach din 4 — somewhat like Tzitz Eliezer; but one may say there for taharot it is different end quote. And he z"l wrote that Rashi never intended this at all, only wrote so for another intent; and in the case of the question he raised that if she now says it is clear to me I checked well, one may be lenient end quote.]`,
    '11#_': `If she found a stain. See responsum Tiferet Tzvi Yoreh Deah siman 24, who wrote specifically if she found the stain within the first three days she does not attribute; but if she found the stain after three days, only there is concern that this stain she saw within three days and did not know until now — she attributes to her wound and her niddah and counts also the first three days for counting — not like one rav who erred in this end quote; and so it appears in responsum A.A.Z. Pnei Me'orot part 2 siman 170 end quote; however in responsum Beit Efrayim, which I will cite below in name of his brother, it is clear his view is not so [and examine this in Sha"ch above siman 190 seif 8 and responsum Chemdat Shlomo siman 20, what he wrote at length].`,
    '12#_': `One does not attribute it to be lenient. [See responsum Chemdat Shlomo siman 20, who raised that if the stain is found on a garment that does not accept tumah, even within three days we are not concerned, since something that does not accept tumah is not included in the decree of stains; and so from words of Hagahot Maimoniyot; and in Sefer HaTaharah they wrote only that within three days we do not attribute — meaning in something that requires attributing properly we say it is more reasonable to attribute to her body; but regarding something that does not accept tumah, we do not come to attribute because of it — even if she checked the ground of the world and sat on it she is pure; and this is since without sensation she sees, the Sages did not decree on this; if so, what does it help that within three days her opening is open — ultimately she sees without sensation. And again I saw in responsum Me'il Tzedakah siman 62 that from his words it appears simple to permit stains found on dyed clothing within three days end quote.]`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#ד': `Even if the congregation accepted Shabbat. For that addition does not relate to niddah matters and requires twenty-four hours; and so it states in chapter HaNa'arah (Ketubot page 47a) in Tosafot s.v. demasar lah on Shabbatot and Yom Tov; and there is proof from counting the omer that we do not consider night even on erev Shabbat, for tosefet Shabbat is from Torah; and likewise eating matzah on Pesach and sukkah end quote — the Agur in name of Maharil; from his words and from all these proofs it appears that even though she also prayed and accepted Shabbat it is permitted (and so is the view of one of the great ones in a responsum that Tashbetz brought there, that even if she prayed Maariv it is permitted — see there in Tashbetz); and per this, what the Rav wrote that even if the congregation accepted Shabbat, etc. — even if the congregation also accepted Shabbat and she too — the Gaon Ammo"z"l did not write so in his responsum, rather wrote for the Rav that if the prayer is Maariv one may not be lenient even bediavad, as below siman 375 end quote (and so is Maharshal's view in responsum siman 72 that I cited below s.k. 19) — it is clear from the Agur I cited that even for those who are lenient it is only until twenty-four hours; and if so, when the day is so long we consider day and night equal, as for example siman 184 s.k. 7); but after that, for all it is forbidden.`,
    '2#_': `Only day one. And her opening is open; and it is written in Sefer Ma'adanei Melekh page 300a, and this is the language: and you will ask — for us what practical difference? Behold even a woman who sees a stain — the custom is she must wait five days with the day she saw, and afterward stops and counts seven clean days (as below siman 11); and it appears there is practical difference for us, such as a bride who is permitted immediately when she stops to count seven clean days; and see Shach, who wrote in name of Hagahot Maimoniyot another practical difference: if she returned and saw within the counting days — since she saw only one day within the counting days, morning checking does not help even bediavad; but if she saw only a stain, one may be lenient both within counting days and at the beginning end quote.`,
    '4#_': `But once. Within the seven clean days, aside from the checking of hefsek taharah above seif 1.`,
    '10#ב': `Some say in three, etc. Thus wrote D"M in Mordechai: if she counts within seven clean days, it is reasonable from three days onward stains are pure if there is something to attribute to wound, bruise, or bird, and she does not forbid; but she must know with certainty that source blood stopped completely — therefore the first three days of counting clean days must be completely pure (and in Mordechai there it states from Sefer HaTaharah — I abbreviated) end quote; and so in Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 9 from Fathers of Impurity; and it appears that not attributing to wound in the first three days is specifically regarding a wound not known to discharge blood — we attribute other stains as above siman 187 (seif 5), and so from Mordechai's language that it deals with this; but regarding a wound known to discharge blood, it appears we attribute even in the first three days, for even regarding actual sight we attribute above siman 187 to a wound discharging blood — all the more rabbinic stains; and one may say actual sight is different, for it is beginning of sight, therefore we attribute to wound and say her opening is still closed and she did not see; but within her counting her opening is already open — we do not attribute to wound at all — this is not so, for behold I wrote above siman 187 that some hold at her veset time we attribute to wound, since vestot are rabbinic; if so, stains too, which are rabbinic, we attribute to wound; and even for one stringent at veset time, that is because there is one who says vestot are from Torah; but stains, which are rabbinic, all attribute to a wound known to discharge blood, as above, to lean lenient, because other poskim did not distinguish between a stain seen during counting days and other stains; if so, it implies he holds even within three days we attribute a stain wherever there is something to attribute; and so above siman 190 (seif 41) in name of Beit Yosef in name of Rashba, that we attribute a stain even during her counting days; and possibly Sefer HaTaharah and Mordechai were stringent only as a general stringency, for behold Rashba wrote in Teshuvot siman 249: the reason we do not attribute in the first three days is since it is possible to be careful, etc.; and if it were forbidden by law, it would not be relevant to distinguish because one can be careful — rather certainly they were stringent only because one can be careful; but where impossible to be careful, one should not be stringent; and therefore he permitted a stain smaller than a gris, and further we attribute to a louse — so it appears one should not be stringent regarding a wound known to discharge blood end quote D"M; and all his words contradict what he wrote, and it appears to me we do not attribute, etc., and so is Mordechai's language, etc.; on the contrary, the simple meaning of Mordechai's words, which conclude for her, implies he deals with every wound; and further, behold even if she was occupied with bird blood we do not attribute, even though blood is before us — all the more a wound discharging blood; and for the Rav one must distinguish, for bird blood is from another place, unlike a wound on her body where impossible to be careful — and this is not the simple meaning; and further, behold these are words of Sefer HaTaharah and Hagahot Maimoniyot, and the simple meaning of Sefer HaTaharah and Hagahot Maimoniyot implies even from a wound known to discharge blood we do not attribute, for they wrote: if she has a wound in that place she attributes her stain to it even if she does not know whether it discharges blood or not, etc.; and if she counts seven clean days, etc. — therefore the first three days of counting must be clean end quote; and since they wrote at the beginning "even if she does not know her wound discharges blood," it implies all the more when she knows; and if so, since the beginning deals in all cases, the end also deals in all cases; and further, since the poskim attributed the reason that she must know with certainty source blood stopped, it implies in the first three days when she does not know with certainty her source blood stopped, in every case we do not attribute to wound — and so it appears explicitly in Beit Yosef siman 190 in name of Orchot Chayyim; and thus wrote Rambam: a woman afflicted with boils — it is simple her stains are pure, for since boils and sores are in her certainly discharge blood and stain her cloths and garment, and even at her veset time, and all the more during counting days we attribute; and Hagahot Alfasi and Hagahot Maimoniyot commented on him: after three for counting, but within three we do not attribute end quote; and these words of Rambam and Alfasi are in Tashbetz siman 481 — therefore even though certainly they discharge blood, we do not attribute within three; and one may say what the Rav wrote to distinguish between opening closed or open is certainly correct, and so it appears from what the poskim wrote that she must know with certainty source blood stopped; and what he wrote this is not so, for behold I wrote above there are those who hold, etc. — it is not a contradiction, for nevertheless there it is not known her opening is open, unlike here; and further, behold there we hold at veset time we do not attribute to her wound even if known to discharge blood, as written there s.k. 26; and what the Rav wrote is because there is one who holds vestot are from Torah, etc. — certainly not so, for certainly Rashbag, who holds vestot are rabbinic, also holds thus from the proof that otherwise she would never be tamei, as written there in that s.k.; and more correct for the Rav to say that even one stringent there, that is because if not so she would never be tamei, unlike here; but all this is not the simple meaning — rather the opposite is reasonable: here, since her opening is open, all require she know with certainty source blood stopped; and what he wrote thus it appears to lean lenient because other poskim did not distinguish, etc. — this is not worth being lenient, for in many places we find such, even where some poskim are lenient and other poskim were silent — we say learn the silent from the explicit, all the more here where the explicit ones are stringent; and further, who is greater than Sefer HaTaharah and Mordechai and Hagahot Maimoniyot, and all the more Alfasi and Orchot Chayyim and Tashbetz agree to their words; and so Agudah chapter Tinoket siman 39; and what he wrote and so above siman 190 in name of Rashba, etc. — one may say Rashba deals after three and Rashba's words are not better than Rambam's words, who wrote during counting days we attribute to be lenient, and Alfasi explained after three; and even if Rashba disputes this he is alone against all these great ones, especially since they are stringent; and what he wrote and possibly they were stringent only as a general stringency, for behold Rashba wrote in Teshuvot siman 249, etc. — I do not know what proof this is, for Tashbetz did not write there except smaller than a gris, and further we attribute to a louse since it is very common and impossible to be careful — if so there is no woman who can count seven clean days, unlike a wound; but certainly the reason is because she must certainly know source blood stopped; again I found in Bach end siman 190, who wrote regarding a woman afflicted with boils: since blood always issues from the boils on her body and stains her cloths and garment, one may attribute even in the first three days, for otherwise she cannot count seven clean days ever; and one may say similarly for a bruise on her body discharging blood; and even so we do not attribute in the first three days to bruise A, which was covered with plaster that does not discharge blood except sometimes — similar to a wound in that place, for Sefer HaTaharah wrote sometimes it discharges blood; but one afflicted with boils, that every hour blood issues from her, is like food-blood that we attribute even in the first three days, etc.; and in any case everything depends on the nature of the boils — examine end quote; it is clear from his words one may be lenient only when full of boils and sores and blood always issues from her; if not so, even if known her wound discharges blood one may not be lenient; and so in Sefer Ephi Rabrevi, who copied all the matters in this siman — only this law that the Rav wrote regarding a wound discharging blood we attribute even in first three days he omitted, only wrote anonymously that one does not attribute a stain like a gris, and nothing else in the first three days end quote.`,
    '11#ו': `Until day six, etc. And Maharshal wrote in responsum siman 82: per this custom, if she saw after Maariv on day one while it is still day, she can wear clean garments at the end of day five and count from day six, and there is no doubt in the matter (and from his words it appears that even though she also prayed Maariv); but per the custom of places of Taz, who wear whites on day (1) [4] of their sight and begin counting from day five — difficult in the matter, etc., and it is not like what Mahar"i wrote in responsum that a woman cannot stop in purity after the congregation prayed Maariv, for here is different since the cause came from Heaven, and it is not relevant to say she should have preceded or delayed, unlike hefsek taharah, etc.; and nevertheless it appears sufficient for us here that we are stringent where she already prayed Maariv and made it already night; but where she did not pray, even if the congregation already prayed Maariv, we are not so stringent to consider it night, since the cause came from Heaven, etc. end quote; and it appears this follows Mahar"i's words above seif 1; but per my example s.k. 4, that Maharil's and the Rav's view in hefsek taharah is to be lenient even if she prayed, and all the more here — a fortiori.`,
    '11#ח': `And every woman who sees a stain, etc. It appears regarding a stain one may be lenient where she did not have relations, to count from the morrow of finding the stain, for this not having relations itself is a distant decree; and also Beit Yosef wrote in his place they did not practice this decree; therefore one may say also Tashbetz himself, who wrote to be stringent, meant regarding sight but not rabbinic stains, for we follow lenient approach in many places, since he did not mention stain at all; and possibly for this the Rav did not write this; and some wrote now, etc., after law of stain only after law of sight; but Taz wrote some wrote that every woman, even seeing a stain like mustard (it appears to emend: or a drop of blood like mustard, but a stain requires a gris, and further as above siman 190), whether she had relations or not — must wait, etc.; and so is the custom.`,
    '12#א': `Must wait, etc. Lest she discharge in three days after relations; and one may say that even if she discharges on day two, in any case it is negation, for after seven it is not negation; one may say we are concerned perhaps she will also discharge on day one after relations — if so there will not be seven consecutive clean days and it will not be negation after seven — a fortiori; and it appears here we do not practice being stringent because of decree of bein hashemashot, and she immerses immediately after four days and one clean day, namely after five days, for uncommon that she errs in counting, and in uncommon matter the Sages did not decree.`,
    '12#ב': `After seven, etc. And in Beit Yosef wrote in name of Rosh that if she immersed on night seven and had relations on day seven, also it is not negation, and she immerses immediately after seven, since she discharged when day seven dawned; and wrote: if we come to reconcile words of Semak to Rosh, one may say what Semak wrote negation after seven is not negation means after night seven end quote; and difficult practically — the simple meaning of Semak implies only after seven specifically.`,
    '13#_': `And so is the custom, as already explained, etc. I do not know what relevance this has to that, for there expertise is not relevant at all, and we decree lest she had relations as if she had relations; and likewise regarding a woman, bathing and drying do not help, for they are not better than having relations at all; and there we decree in a common matter; but nevertheless there is practical difference in bathing and drying in the law mentioned in the previous seif, forgetting one day that she must wait six onot — bathing and drying help, for she counts immediately after she completes the day she forgot — examine; and it appears the Rav also agrees to this and deals with an ordinary woman; and nevertheless for the law, in any case, since Semak and Hagahot Maimoniyot wrote we are not expert, one may not be lenient lechatchila; and so Bach.`,
  },
  'tiferet-yisrael': {
    '1#_': `Siman 61 in Shulchan Aruch siman 1. And they consider this bediavad; per my humble opinion I say, to resolve Tosafot's question on Shach siman 375: why regarding mourning that day did not count — because here if we are stringent to consider night, sometimes there is also a leniency, as explained s.k. 89: a woman established a veset three times and is not concerned further until uprooted three times, and all stains she attributes to be lenient; and likewise if uprooted three times she is no longer concerned; and it is clear it must be specifically in one onah — equal day or night; and if so, if a woman sees twice at night on twentieth of the month and on third saw on Yom Tov while still a long day after the congregation prayed Maariv — if we consider it night, this woman establishes on the tenth a veset and need not be concerned for any other veset; and similarly if she establishes veset by day and uprooted it twice at night and on third saw by day after Maariv prayer — if we consider it night it is uprooting and she is permitted even in that onah she was accustomed to before, for uprooting as required by law; and in this matter there would be a great leniency; and therefore they consider it day, for in truth it is still a long day, unlike mourning where we are properly stringent because no leniency emerges — a fortiori; and one may reject that also regarding mourning a leniency is relevant for distant hearing — if it is night it is after thirty days.`,
    '2#_': `Seif 1 in Shulchan Aruch siman 1 in the Hagahah. Since the congregation already made it night; and per my humble opinion I rely on what R. Yehudah said in the mishnah, that she must check from minchah and upward; and Beit Yosef already wondered: the day is still long two and a half hours — what is this great measure from night that he mentioned? Indeed R. Yehudah according to his reasoning holds minchah until plag minchah; and see Rashi to Berakhot, who holds from there his law is like night for all matters; and if so hefsek taharah does not help; and therefore he gave time from minchah and upward; and per this they already said in Berakhot the halachah was not stated whether like R. Yehudah or like the Sages — one who acts like this acts, and one who acts like that acts; if so the congregation who prayed Maariv while still day took the essence like R. Yehudah, that it is night — if so how can she stop in purity? What for R. Yehudah does not help — examine.`,
    '4#_': `Seif 3 there in the Hagahah. And they consider this bediavad; and what is below siman 375 — the Mechaber and Rama did not emend that one may not count within seven end quote Shach there; and one may say here if we are stringent to consider night it is a stringency that leads to leniency — needless to say in Talmudic times before R. Zeira's stringency: if on day three of her zivah she saw only after Maariv — if you consider it night there is no zavah gedolah here and she observes one day against one day; unlike if it is day, behold she saw three consecutive days and she is zavah gedolah; and if so the law did not move from its place; and one may say also for us: if she saw blood and there is something to attribute to a wound — if one considers night, behold she was presumed for three days she did not see and attributes; unlike if they consider day, behold she was not presumed to have stopped seeing three days and she is forbidden; and therefore they are not stringent, for the stringency is a leniency — examine.`,
    '5#_': `Seif 4 in Shulchan Aruch siman 1. The seven clean days must be consecutive, etc.; and the question of Taz: behold after R. Zeira's stringency there is no practical difference whether she negates or not; and it appears there is practical difference if counting of seven clean days of zivah continues within blood of purity — for counting seven clean days of zavah, blood of purity does not help, for we require completely clean, as Taz wrote and as stated siman 194; unlike if we say she does not negate but only has a new sight — behold it is blood of purity, and simple; and even though the custom is to refrain from relations on blood of purity, nevertheless many laws have practical difference whether forbidden by law or because of custom; and likewise regarding attributing to it, as explained above siman 190.`,
    '6#_': `Seif 5 there in the Hagahah. Some say in the first three days — I am astonished at the body of this law: is it not clear in the Gemara that if she checked herself on the second for sight and found no blood, and did not check again until day eight and found tamei — we do not defile retroactively, for we establish her in presumption of purity, and she was not presumed for three days stopping; learn from this that with one checking and not finding she was presumed; and further, if so you presume her in presumption of seeing — that which they say therefore for Rabbi she is not pure, she attributes observing one day against one day, for they are equivalent; and Rashi explained she too is in presumption of purity; and difficult — behold she was not presumed to stop three days; consider yourself: she the pure one can attribute to stain, wound, and observing one day against one day — impossible to attribute; and how are they equivalent — behold she is in presumption of impurity so much that I attribute to her more than a wound; and how did you pair that she attributes to wound — she cannot attribute for Rabbi; and even Rashbag, who disagrees, from reason of attributing the mishap to the defective, but not from the above reason; and it would appear to say Mordechai and Sefer HaTaharah deal with one who saw three days, therefore she needs three days she did not see to remove presumption; but if she saw only two days she does not need, and hefsek suffices — all the more; but his language does not imply so; and also if she checked herself on day four, why also do we presume presumption of purity? And in the Gemara it appears she negates presumption of purity on day four from day three, two; and therefore it must be that certainly per what I stated siman 50 regarding doubtful life — presumption is not relevant since there is presumption of prohibition; even though in slaughter it was permitted, nevertheless we do not say presumption; and likewise here — behold otherwise tamei; and even though in this there would be permission in this counting, nevertheless there is no presumption; and if so all stains one should be stringent; only we say since blood should not come from a woman we attribute to stain; but this one who was not presumed three days — still common in her — we attribute to something common, as above siman 153 in Shach; and if so Rama"ch wrote well regarding a wound discharging blood: more common than blood from source; and behold she is permitted; and behold it is without sensation, and it is a rabbinic doubt for leniency, since both are common; and in vain the later authorities were stringent regarding her wound discharging blood, for the above law contradicts.`,
    '7#_': `Seif 6 in Shulchan Aruch siman 1. One who discharges, etc. — six onot, the first in Orach Chayyim challenged: if so, on four he makes separation; if so on seven in Sivan the Torah was given — how do we say time of giving Torah on sixth of Sivan? (And one need not say time fifty for counting, for some say for counting the Torah was given — thus one must interpret his words.) And the Yerushalmi chapter Re'eh was hidden from him, for it is clear there: six onot he does not learn from Sinai at all, only from this gezeirah shavah: "this is the law of zav" and "that which issues from him" — Sha"z: just as zav three, so Sha"z three; and he holds one does not learn generations from Sinai, as explained there in the matter of R. Yochanan; and one may say that even on five he makes separation — there is different, for revealed before the Omnipresent those who had relations on day four and night will not discharge at all; and for generations we learn from that gezeirah shavah of zav — examine.`,
    '9#_': `Seif 8 in Shulchan Aruch 13. To discharge all seed and it is not forbidden because of wasting seed — Tosafot were puzzled regarding relations with a cloth; and these matters are an arranged mishnah; and it must be that even though in three days for absorption most seed fit for pregnancy is absorbed immediately, and the minority absorbed after two or three days — what is absorbed will not help at all from bathing, since the womb already absorbed; and if so permitted to bathe, for what was not absorbed, on the majority it will not be absorbed further, and there is no wasting of seed here — permitted; and this was in hidden manner from the Rav, first in laws of Yom Kippur, who wondered that they permitted a woman to bathe, for behold it is wasting seed — see there.`,
  },
  'torat-hashlamim': {
    '1#ב': `Since the congregation already made it night. Even though she did not pray so — Tashbetz wrote, and it is simple: if it is night or even doubt of night, and she did not check, even though she and the congregation have not yet prayed — nevertheless she cannot count from the morrow, since it is already night, and we require seven complete clean days and each day twenty-four hours, as clear from words of Agur and meaning of poskim, that there is no distinction between Tammuz and Av or Kislev and Tevet — forever she must check near bein hashemashot, and the measure is explained in Orach Chayyim siman 261, that it is walking one thousand and five hundred amot before night — unlike the implication of Shach s.s. 4 that there is distinction between days end quote.`,
    '2#_': `Only day one. And even for us, who practice that even a woman who saw a stain must wait five days and afterward stops, as below seif 11 — nevertheless there is practical difference for us, such as a bride who is permitted immediately when she stops to count seven clean days; and see Shach, who wrote in name of Hagahot Maimoniyot another practical difference: if she returned and saw within her counting days — if she saw only one day, morning checking does not help even bediavad; but if she saw only a stain, one may be lenient both within counting days and at the beginning end quote.`,
    '4#_': `And not by candlelight. And that which is stated at the beginning of Pesachim: candlelight is good for checking; and even a portico where light is great — one should not check lechatchila by daylight; and as explained in Orach Chayyim siman 433 — one may say it is different regarding checking for chametz, which requires checking holes and cracks in a place daylight cannot reach, unlike by candle one can search holes and cracks; unlike checking the garment and cloth and the like, which can go in an exposed place — therefore better to check by daylight; and further one may say the color of the stain is recognized more by day than at night by candlelight (unlike chametz, which is a physical object); and this is the reason also in negaim, that they are seen only by day; and see Ramban chapter 9 of Laws of Impurity of Tzara'at.`,
    '6#_': `Until the place the sun compresses. And see responsum Meshech Chokhmah siman 49 on the matter of checking; and in Ma'adanei Melekh page 301a.`,
    '10#ב': `And you know it discharges blood. Shach elaborated to challenge this law and wrote one may be lenient only if the wound always discharges blood; and in truth his words are not compelled at all; and regarding stains we hear to be lenient as explained above siman 190; and so responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 65: thus wrote one who is lenient when she found a stain within three of seven clean days to attribute even for any reason does not lose so much, since in any case many are lenient in stains within three end quote; and it appears if she found a stain within first three days of seven clean days she must return and count seven clean days anew; and then even if she saw a stain within three of the second seven clean days she attributes to be lenient, since already the first three days passed and the second time she did not see from her body; and if you say it was from her body — her opening was not presumed open from sight of the stain; and further it is a doubt within a doubt — doubt whether the first stain was from her body, and if you say from her body, perhaps the second stain was not from her body — examine; and see Taz, who wrote that per the Rav one may not attribute to a wound unless impossible to be careful not to soil herself; but if possible to be careful and she did not do so — one does not attribute.`,
    '11#א': `Six complete onot. So too Tosafot and Rosh in chapter Bnot Kutim, because the halachah is like the Sages in chapter Re'eh that we require six complete onot; but difficult on Rosh, who on Shabbat chapter Re'eh quotes simply the anonymous mishnah: one who discharges semen on day three is pure — and how well did Rif do, who copied the second version in the Gemara: from where for one who discharges Sha"z that she is tamei; and similarly difficult on Agudah, who on Shabbat ruled explicitly on day three pure, and in chapter Bnot Kutim ruled like Tosafot and Rosh; and further difficult to me that Rambam in chapter 5 of Laws of Fathers of Impurity ruled after three onot pure, and wrote there Hagahot Maimoniyot he ruled like R. Eliezer that one who discharges on day three is pure, and he reads in Gemara Shabbat "but the Sages say three onot" — and this is Rashba's version (and difficult, for Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba reads explicitly six complete onot and ruled so; and if the intent of Rav R. Shimshon bar Avraham, who is Rosh of Sens, he also causes the opposite, as written in commentary on mishnayot chapter 8 of Mikvaot — difficult); and now it is resolved that he ruled like the Sages end quote Hagahot Maimoniyot there; and if so difficult that he omitted his view entirely in Beit Yosef and here in Shulchan Aruch — difficult.`,
    '11#ד': `Without having relations. Even if her husband is in the city — so Shach and Taz, unlike Bach; and Shach wrote: and greater than this they practice that even a bride does not count seven clean days until day five of her sight, even though the groom has not yet come to the city; even though per my view there is no reason to be stringent at all, nor mentioned in any early or late posek — nevertheless let it be only among permitted matters where others practiced prohibition, etc.; and nevertheless in time of pressure one may permit a bride that immediately when she stops in purity she counts seven clean days — for thus is better than the custom that they marry while niddah end quote; and it appears this "time of pressure" that Shach used literally means such as they already slaughtered the feast and the like, in a matter impossible to delay the wedding until she becomes pure — then better to count the seven clean days immediately so she is not married while niddah — a fortiori; and see Taz s.k. 7, that it is clear in his words one should not be stringent at all regarding a bride, even a widow after her chuppah, as long as she has not yet entered complete relations with him; and law of bride after relations of mitzvah — see above siman 193 and in Magen Avraham this siman s.k. 5.`,
    '11#ו': `And one should not change. And Shelah wrote page 101a, thus: an incident came before me with a woman on day four of her niddah, but she did not have relations with her husband on the night before her sight — and I permitted her to wear whites on day four and begin counting on day five; and my reason was: even though Terumat HaDeshen was stringent not to begin counting until day six of her sight, and we are stringent stringency upon stringency even though she did not have relations before her sight — nevertheless in this incident I did not wish to be stringent, for it is a stringency that leads to leniency: if she had begun counting on day six, then her night of immersion would fall on the night of Shabbat after Yom Tov; better to leave the above stringency in order to bring the washing close to immersion and she immerses on the night of Yom Tov end quote; and likewise even though one should not change the custom — nevertheless where there is room to be lenient without relations one should not be stringent; and in detail, as I already wrote above ch"q 15, that some great poskim hold even with relations she does not negate what she discharges after three onot.`,
    '12#א': `To wait six onot and one clean day and immerse immediately after five days. And since the Rav did not write here also the decree of bein hashemashot as above seif 11, it implies here we do not decree so much, because it is an uncommon matter; and so Shach.`,
    '13#_': `For we are not expert nowadays. And nevertheless Derishah wrote end siman 7, thus: and I heard from my teacher that a woman should not enter within three days after her immersion into a bathhouse; and it appears this is from the reason that they said a woman conceives only near her veset or after her immersion; and the bathhouse causes discharge of seed; and per this one must be careful also not to walk much after relations per Ramban's view end quote; and it must be that he did not warn from this reason also not to enter the bathhouse during relations near her veset — one may say because he holds in any case: if she established a veset and knows her veset time — then forbidden to have relations; and if she does not know her veset time and afterward saw — also permitted to bathe, for if she were pregnant she would have ceased blood — a fortiori; and per this if it is such a case where permitted to have relations near her veset as above siman 184 — also forbidden to bathe within three of this relations, or until she sees blood — a fortiori; and as stated above siman 184 s.k. 7, unlike Derishah — a fortiori.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '10#א': `Must be consecutive, etc. In Beit Yosef it is written that from this there is no practical difference except for Talmudic law, where there was distinction between niddah and zivah; but nowadays, after R. Zeira's stringency that every woman who sees even a drop like mustard must sit seven clean days, there is no practical difference from this law; for even if she does not negate, behold she must sit seven clean days because of the sight now — and this is a great stretch that Tur would write law according to the Talmud also in Shulchan Aruch; Beit Yosef and Rama wrote, and therefore there is some practical difference from it; and it appears to me there is practical difference even for us: per what we hold, a woman who has a veset need not check at all, as written beginning siman 187 — the reason is then she is in presumption of purity, unlike regarding seven clean days — we hold here lechatchila she needs checking, the reason being she is in presumption of impurity until the seven clean days emerge; therefore he informs us here presumption of impurity is upon her until seven consecutive are completed; lest you say at end of seven clean days behold she returns to presumption of purity and need not check even lechatchila — he informs us even if she sees at the very end of the seven she negates everything; if so still her original presumption is upon her and needs checking lechatchila also at that time, per my humble opinion.`,
    '10#ב': `But less than a gris, etc. In Beit Yosef in name of Tashbetz wrote thus: but in less than that she need not attribute it to bruise or wound, only to food-blood — certainly we attribute forever, for otherwise no woman can become pure, as stated chapter HaRo'eh: there is no woman upon whom there are not several drops of food-blood end quote; and Rama wrote in D"M: that not attributing to bruise is certainly a general stringency, for if by law what do we care whether impossible to purify or not; and he proved from law we attribute even in first three days, for even regarding actual sight we attribute to wound she has in that place; and he concludes there: so it appears to me for halachah; but practically one should be stringent lechatchila regarding passing wound in few days, for also this is called possible to be careful end quote; and nevertheless he wrote here to permit regarding a wound she knows with certainty discharges blood; and it appears what he brought in D"M proof from actual sight is not proof, for there she has initial presumption of purity and you come to defile her — therefore we attribute to wound; unlike here where she is already tamei and you need to purify her — therefore you need clear presumption for purity to remove her from previous presumption of impurity; therefore we require three completely clean days — then there is presumption for purity in three days; but in less than a gris impossible in the world to be careful in this — the Sages did not decree, for this matter is only rabbinic, since she does not feel blood issuing, as above; and likewise that case of one afflicted with boils that I copied end siman 190 — impossible to be careful from it; and so it appears regarding a wound she has on her body — whenever she can be careful not to soil herself from blood and did not do so, she may not attribute to that wound; unlike a wound she cannot guard properly — she attributes; and Rama's words here in Shulchan Aruch also intend this. And Moharach z"l copied in name of Bedek HaBayit on Beit Yosef, who wrote on that case of one afflicted with boils in name of Alfasi: in first three days one does not attribute to boils; and Moharach z"l concludes everything depends on the boils — namely per the manner I wrote.`,
    '11#א': `Semen does not putrefy, etc. Explanation: and after it putrefies, even though she discharges it, behold she is like one discharging mere water; therefore they required she wait six complete onot — meaning each onah is measure of day or night, namely twelve hours; and therefore if she had relations on night of relations of mitzvah, for example two hours at night, and begins counting seven clean days from day four — behold six onot were not completed until night four two hours into it; and if so how can she count day four as a clean day, for perhaps she will discharge in those two hours at beginning of night and semen is still not putrefied; and every clean day must be entirely clean both at night and on the day within it — therefore she should not begin counting except from day five; all who had relations on the night after Shabbat, except there is one more stringency: namely if she had relations on night of relations of mitzvah — if we permit her to count from day five, she may err if she has relations on the first day during its bein hashemashot and she thinks it is still day — it turns out per this her vessels: six onot of hers at end of day four and the night after it would be for beginning clean days, since still day, and we count hour for hour, and she begins counting from the fifth day; but in truth it was then night and her six onot were not completed until beginning of night of day five; and perhaps she will discharge Sha"z at that beginning of night — it turns out she must count from day six, for day five is not clearly clean from discharge of Sha"z at its beginning; therefore the later authorities were stringent regarding every woman who has relations on whatever day — we consider her as if she had relations on the day after it; therefore if she had relations on night of relations of mitzvah she counts from day six as if she had relations at end of day one during bein hashemashot, when she is compelled to count from day six from force of doubt; and they placed this stringency upon her for having relations all that first day, even beginning of its night; and I already wrote siman 193 in name of Maharal of Prague that a bride after relations of mitzvah can count from day five for her relations — his intent to say we do not decree upon her lest she have relations that day at its end, namely during bein hashemashot; and his reason appears: since here there is no niddah blood, only hymeneal blood, they were not stringent because of relations during bein hashemashot; but if she truly had relations of mitzvah during bein hashemashot, it appears certainly we consider her as if she had relations on the night after that bein hashemashot, even regarding a bride — it turns out if the bride had relations on night of relations of mitzvah she counts from day five; and if she had relations during bein hashemashot at end of day one she counts from day six, per my humble opinion.`,
    '11#ב': `And there is no reason in the matter. Moharach z"l brought a reason for the matter in name of Rosh of Austria: since niddah from Torah immerses after seven days of niddah even if they were not clean days, only she requires seven clean days lest she be in zivah days — and in early days they practiced immersing twice: once after seven from Torah and second after seven clean days; and they remembered that matter and also practiced waiting fourteen days end quote; and Rama wrote well that one who prepares himself prepares for the mitzvah, and such reasons are not sufficient to delay immersion of mitzvah.`,
    '12#ג': `Behold she immerses at any time. For behold she already counted seven clean days, only lacking immersion; and I have great astonishment in this seif from what is written in Even HaEzer siman 13 seif 5: a convert and his wife, when converted, separate them ninety days to distinguish between seed planted in holiness and seed planted not in holiness; and likewise we should separate her after he had relations in prohibition, to distinguish if she conceived in prohibition when she immersed improperly; and it appears here it deals with a woman whose husband was available to her before this — therefore the distinction is not relevant, similar to a woman raped under her husband who need not wait ninety days if her husband had relations with her first, as Rama wrote there Even HaEzer seif 6; and per this, if her husband was not with her before this act within ninety days, automatically she needs separation after this forbidden relations for ninety days — so it appeared to me; and difficult practically; but what I wrote above end siman (193) [192] transgressing the law requiring seven clean days because of anticipation — she need not separate — this is not at all like that case of convert we mentioned, for there is only a rabbinic prohibition alone, as written end siman 192.`,
  },
};
